{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS001267","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 9 candidates are direct matches to the target trait (ankylosing spondylitis) by trait_efo, and most have endpoint-faithful phenotyping_reported. However, several candidates come from framework-style UK Biobank sweeps (snpnet “813 traits” and the “Portability of 245 polygenic scores” paper), and the Taiwanese EAS models report only full-model AUROC with unknown covariates and small validation (n=1,298), limiting cross-model comparability.

Among the models with PRS-comparable metrics, PGS001267 provides explicit PGS-only AUROC (0.7265) and PGS-only R2 (0.0988) plus full-model AUROC (0.7433) in a large European validation (n=67,425) with basic covariates (age, sex, array, PCs). Its phenotype is a generic ankylosing spondylitis diagnosis, aligning cleanly with the target, whereas PGS001268 uses a time-to-event endpoint (a mild endpoint mismatch penalty for a generic disease target). The UKB portability models (PGS001876/PGS002089) lack AUROC/R2 and offer only partial-r, making them harder to compare directly here.

Primary limitation: the selected best model (PGS001267) is from a pan-trait UKB snpnet framework with a very sparse 10-variant score, which domain guidance flags as potentially less reliable/portable despite good internal metrics; no clearly disease-focused multi-cohort ankylosing spondylitis PRS is present in this context to resolve that concern, so confidence cannot be high."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005262","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates are present for the target trait (nodular goiter), including (i) nontoxic multinodular goiter scores (PGS001814, PGS002022) and (ii) benign nodular goiter scores (PGS005261–PGS005263). Models contrasting thyroid carcinoma vs benign nodular goiter (PGS005273–PGS005274) are less endpoint-faithful to a generic nodular goiter target because they are case-control contrasts centered on malignancy status.

Among the benign nodular goiter direct matches, PGS005262 has the strongest visible performance evidence in its sibling cluster (highest reported full-model AUROC 0.5933 vs 0.5854 and 0.5876 for PGS005261/PGS005263) and is from a disease-focused multi-biobank study family (thyroid cancer and benign thyroid diseases), which domain policy indicates should generally outrank portability/framework-derived scores when endpoint alignment is acceptable. Compared with the UKB portability scores (PGS001814/PGS002022), which report only modest partial correlations (~0.024–0.028) from a pan-trait portability paper, the PGS005262 package provides both a direct benign nodular goiter endpoint and supportive effect-size evidence (OR per SD 1.408) alongside large multi-ancestry evaluation (n=94,651).

Limitation: for PGS005262 the covariates are listed as Unknown and the available discrimination metric is a full-model AUROC (not explicitly PRS-only), which domain guidance warns should not be over-interpreted; this keeps confidence below High and makes the overall direct-match recommendation sub-optimal rather than clearly high-quality."}
